                <pb facs="0812" n="692" sameAs="frus:frus1905/pg692"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Garrett to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Hague,
                                Netherlands</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-11-02"><hi rendition="#italic">November 2,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 800.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Mr. Newel’s No. 790, of
                    October 3, in connection with the meeting of the arbitral tribunal in the
                    Japanese house-tax case, I have the honor to state that the secretary-general of
                    the Permanent Court of Arbitration informs me that the first meeting of the
                    tribunal will take place at The Hague on the 21st instant; and, further, that
                    the Governments of France, Germany, and Great Britain have collectively
                    designated the following gentlemen as their agents to form a joint delegation to
                    appear before the tribunal, viz: <list>
                        <item>The Government of France: Mr. Fromageot, LL. D., advocate at the court
                            of appeal at Paris.</item>
                        <item>The Government of Germany: Mr. Weipert, unattached consul.</item>
                        <item>The Government of Great Britain: Mr. Ambrose B. Walford.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John W. Garrett.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
